verdict.betterFix:
Replace the recursion at :236 with a per-class least fixpoint, and drop the "in-progress treated as false" fallback, because it is unsound.

```ts
/** Per class: which readable member bodies may start a navigation, by least fixpoint over this.member() calls (cycles start false and only grow). */
const navigatingBodies = new WeakMap<ClassDeclaration, Map<Node, boolean>>()
function bodyMayNavigate(body: Node, cls: ClassDeclaration): boolean {
  let known = navigatingBodies.get(cls)
  if (!known) {
    known = new Map()
    const bodies = cls.getMembers().map((m) => calledMemberBodyOf(m)).filter((b): b is Node => !!b)
    for (const b of bodies) known.set(b, false)
    navigatingBodies.set(cls, known)
    for (let changed = true; changed;) {
      changed = false
      for (const b of bodies) if (!known.get(b) && mayNavigate(b, cls, known)) { known.set(b, true); changed = true }
    }
  }
  return known.get(body) ?? true
}
```

- In `mayNavigate`, replace the `visiting` parameter with the `known` map. At :236, for a resolved `body`, return `known.get(body)` instead of recursing.
- The loop runs at most as many passes as there are members, each linear in the class size, so the whole thing is O(members x class size) instead of 2^k.
- Callers outside the loop (:287, :291) call `bodyMayNavigate`.

For `after` (:272-305):
- Build the caller index once per class, as a `Map<string, CallExpression[]>`, instead of `getDescendantsOfKind` on every step (:300).
- Memoise on (site, valueWaits). Cache a false result only when the computation never hit the `visiting.has(site)` cut (:273). True results are always safe to cache.
- A false that came from a cycle cut means "not superseded". Caching it can produce a spurious must edge, which is the same unsoundness as the rejected fallback.

